## Security-Engineer Output — Immutable Audit Log System (AUDIT-2026-06-11) — 2026-06-11

STATUS: DONE (0 Critical, 0 unmitigated High — all findings are forward REQUIREMENTS S1-S12; no code exists yet). Pre-implementation threat model.

### STEP 0 — agentmemory recall (GEL-2 verbatim record)
Tool: mcp__plugin_agentmemory_agentmemory__memory_recall. 5 queries run:
- Query "auth security findings" → HIT (overflowed to file). Top relevant: SECURITY_PATTERN OAuth2 Google Login threat model (ADR-036/037/039/040: email_verified gate, back-channel identity, fixed-URL egress NOT SSRF, secret-never-logged); SECURITY_PATTERN URL-Shortener Abuse Prevention; qa P1 fixes (TokenReuseError, audit actorId, generic-message info-disclosure flag); SECURITY_DECISION ADR-036..040.
- Query "IDOR" → HIT (inline). Top: task-mgmt threat model (404-not-403 IDOR enumeration ADR-013, UUID PK IDOR resistance ADR-016); URL-shortener ADR-021 owner-only 404-not-403; backend-dev tasks.policy.ts centralized object-level authz; qa abuse-matrix role-guard/IDOR loop over all 6 admin endpoints + role unforgeability.
- Query "audit" → HIT (overflowed). Top: code-quality AP-4 layered review; backend-dev AP-3; architect ADR-010/011 module boundaries; data-lead models. No prior DB-backed audit table — audit has only ever been the logger emitter (confirms orchestrator finding).
- Query "admin authorization" → HIT (inline). Top: orchestrator URL-abuse routing (admin role server-set + HS256-signed, NEVER from body, no self-promotion); data-lead OAuth google_id @unique; ADR-005 HS256 alg-pin. Confirms requireRole(ADMIN) → 403 pattern (ADR-033).
- Query "PII logging" → HIT (overflowed). Top: qa P1 (info-disclosure generic-message); backend-dev M8 (Pino redaction, lowercase email); data-lead OAuth (google_email is PII, covered by Omit<passwordHash> boundary + redaction; google_id opaque correlator NOT in audit; ADR-040 carries actorId not sub).
NONE empty. No prior DB-backed audit-log findings exist (this is the first audit-table threat model) — generic OWASP + reuse of the established 404/403, redaction, server-derived-context, and immutability disciplines apply.

### CONTEXT BUDGET COMPLIANCE
- brief.md: grepped tags `agent: security-engineer`, `domain: security`, `domain: architecture`, `agent: orchestrator`. Read lines 5858-6032 (orchestrator section + my section + receipts) ≈ 175 of 6091 (file grew to ~6173 mid-session via parallel writes; my section relocated to ~6168). Did NOT read full file.
- decisions.md: recalled relevant ADRs via agentmemory instead of full read (ADR-010/011 boundaries, ADR-012 rotation, ADR-013/016/021 IDOR/404-not-403, ADR-033 role-403, ADR-040 audit-carries-actorId-not-sub, ADR-044 Result). Existing ADRs end at ADR-044; mine start ADR-045.
- src read (pattern confirmation): src/shared/audit.ts, src/shared/logger.ts, src/admin/admin.routes.ts, src/admin/admin.policy.ts, src/shared/auth-context.ts, src/shared/jwt.ts, src/shared/errors.ts (grep). Grepped ip/user-agent/trustProxy across all src.

### REVIEW_SCOPE (Step 0 differential risk classification)
HIGH RISK (full review): src/audit/audit.routes.ts (admin endpoint, query filters), the audit emit call-sites in auth/tasks/urls/admin services (actor/ip provenance), migration 006 (DB immutability trigger), new ip/user-agent extraction.
MEDIUM RISK: src/audit/audit.service.ts, audit.repository.ts (Result pattern, fire-and-forget).
LOW RISK: audit.schemas.ts (Zod filter validation), fake-prisma auditLog delegate.

### ATTACK SURFACE
New endpoint: GET /audit-logs (admin-only, paginated, filters event_type/actor_id/target_id/date-range). New data at rest: audit_logs(actor_id, ip_address, user_agent, event_type, target_id, outcome, metadata JSON, created_at). New write paths: fire-and-forget INSERT from auth/tasks/urls/admin services. New input extraction: client IP + User-Agent (NEITHER exists in src today — grep returned 0 matches). Trust boundary: the audit log is a FORENSIC/SECURITY artifact — its integrity is itself a security control, so the threat model treats the app account as a potential adversary (DB-level immutability).

### THREAT MODEL (STRIDE) + FINDINGS

| Component | STRIDE | Threat |
|-----------|--------|--------|
| GET /audit-logs | Info Disclosure / Elevation | non-admin reads the org-wide audit trail (every user's IP/UA/actions) |
| audit_logs rows | Tampering / Repudiation | compromised app account (or SQLi via app) UPDATEs/DELETEs entries to erase its own tracks |
| metadata JSON | Info Disclosure | secrets/PII (password, tokens, code_verifier, google sub, full bodies) leak into a long-lived queryable store |
| ip_address/actor_id | Spoofing / Repudiation | attacker forges actor or IP via a client-controllable field → false attribution / log injection |
| fire-and-forget emit | Repudiation / DoS | attacker induces audit-INSERT failure to evade logging; or silent swallow hides security events |
| date-range/filters | DoS / Injection | malformed/huge range or raw-SQL interpolation |

#### Findings (ID | Severity | CWE | Title | Exploit Path | Fix / Required Action)

- **F1 | HIGH | CWE-285/639 | Non-admin access to audit trail (admin-only + no self-service IDOR).** Exploit: a regular USER calls GET /audit-logs and reads every user's IP/UA/action history (mass PII + recon), or a `?actor_id=<other-user>` filter lets one user read another's audit trail. Fix: register the audit query route in a plugin with **plugin-wide `app.addHook('preHandler', authGuard)` + `app.addHook('preHandler', requireRole(UserRole.ADMIN))`** — the EXACT pattern in src/admin/admin.routes.ts:91-92. Role failure → ForbiddenError 403 (ADR-033: admin capability is not an enumerable object secret, so honest 403 is correct — do NOT use 404 here). There is **NO per-user self-service read** endpoint — `actor_id` is an admin FILTER, not an ownership scope; the endpoint must NOT branch to "return only my own logs" for non-admins. No object-level IDOR exists because there is exactly one capability gate (admin), not a per-row owner check.

- **F2 | HIGH | CWE-532/312/359 | metadata JSON / IP / UA leaking secrets & PII into a durable queryable store.** Exploit: a careless emit call passes `request.body`, the full token, `code_verifier`, Google `sub`, or a password into `metadata`, persisting it forever in a store admins (and any future breach) can query — far worse than an ephemeral log line. Fix (MANDATE — ADR-046): metadata is a **strict allowlist of non-sensitive identifiers only** (resourceId, outcome, rotation family/jti, count, event-specific enum reasons). metadata may **NEVER** contain: password/passwordHash, access/refresh tokens or any JWT, `code_verifier`/PKCE state, Google `sub`/id_token/`client_secret`, raw request bodies/headers, Authorization/Cookie values, or URL-embedded credentials. Apply the SAME redaction discipline as src/shared/logger.ts REDACT_PATHS (password, passwordHash, refreshToken, accessToken, token, authorization, cookie). Mirror ADR-040: audit carries `actorId`, never the provider `sub`. Recommend the audit service accept only a typed AuditFields-shaped object (never an arbitrary blob) and run a redaction/allowlist pass before INSERT so a caller mistake fails safe. ip_address + user_agent are PII (GDPR Art.4) — see F7 retention.

- **F3 | HIGH | CWE-117/345 | Forged actor / log injection — provenance must be server-derived, never client-controlled.** Exploit: if `actor_id` or `ip_address` is read from the request body/header that the attacker controls, they forge attribution (frame another user) or inject crafted UA/metadata to corrupt the trail (log injection / log forging). Fix: `actor_id` MUST come from `requireAuth(request).userId` (the verified JWT `sub`), or `null` for unauthenticated attempts — NEVER from any body field. `ip_address` MUST be extracted from Fastify **`request.ip`** (which honours the app's `trustProxy: config.isProduction` at src/app.ts:50 — behind the prod proxy it is the real client, locally it is the socket IP; do NOT hand-parse `x-forwarded-for`, spoofable when trustProxy is off). `user_agent` from `request.headers['user-agent']`, length-capped (≤512 chars), stored opaque (data, never interpolated/executed). NOTE: this IP/UA extraction is NET-NEW (grep found zero existing usage) — provide ONE shared helper (e.g. `requestContext(request)` → `{ actorId, ip, userAgent }`) so every call-site is consistent and cannot accidentally trust client input.

- **F4 | HIGH | CWE-778/693 | Immutability of the forensic artifact (DB-level enforcement required).** Exploit: an attacker who reaches the app DB account (compromised creds, or SQLi through any app query) issues `UPDATE/DELETE FROM audit_logs` to erase evidence — app-level "no UPDATE/DELETE endpoint" is NOT sufficient because it shares the app's DB grant. Fix (ADR-045): enforce at BOTH layers. (a) App: NO update/delete endpoints; repository exposes only insert + read. (b) **DB (Postgres): a `BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE ON audit_logs` trigger that `RAISE EXCEPTION`** (or RULE `DO INSTEAD NOTHING`), rejecting even a direct SQL UPDATE/DELETE on the app connection. db-engineer owns the trigger + must verify it on a real Postgres instance (fake-prisma cannot exercise it; qa simulates via an auditLog fake delegate that rejects update/delete). Also: audit rows must **survive user deletion** — actor_id FK must be `ON DELETE SET NULL` (or a plain UUID column with NO cascading FK), so deleting a user CANNOT cascade-delete their audit history (tamper channel + breaks forensics). No User→audit_logs cascade.

- **F5 | MEDIUM | CWE-778/390 | Fire-and-forget must not silently swallow security events.** Exploit: an attacker who can reliably make the audit INSERT fail (e.g. triggering a constraint/connection error during their malicious action) evades logging while the main op still succeeds — and if the failure is swallowed, zero trace. Fix: audit INSERT failure must NOT fail the main op (core feature availability must not depend on the audit write), BUT the catch MUST `request.log.error({ event: 'AUDIT_WRITE_FAILED', action, actorId, err }, ...)` so the failure lands in the application log (→ monitoring/alerting). Residual risk (document, ship w/ mitigation): a capable attacker degrading the DB can still drop audit rows — mitigated by (a) the error-log fallback giving a tamper signal, (b) alerting on AUDIT_WRITE_FAILED rate, (c) the immutability trigger meaning dropped-never-written is the ONLY attack (existing rows can't be altered). Do NOT add a blocking/synchronous audit that would let an attacker DoS the whole API by jamming the audit table.

- **F6 | MEDIUM | CWE-20/400/89 | Filter validation — injection + DoS on the query API.** Exploit: malformed `date_from`/`date_to`, an inverted/absurd range, or unbounded page size causes a full-table scan / memory blowup; raw string interpolation of `event_type`/`actor_id` into SQL would be injectable. Fix: validate ALL filters with Zod (`audit.schemas.ts`): `event_type` ∈ the AuditAction enum values; `actor_id`/`target_id` validated as UUID; date range parsed with `z.coerce.date()` (or `z.string().datetime()`), reject `date_from > date_to`, optionally cap span. Pagination capped at **max 100/page** (reuse existing pagination-cap precedent) with a created_at index backing newest-first ordering. ALL queries via Prisma's parameterized builder — **no `$queryRawUnsafe` / no string-interpolated SQL** with any filter value (Prisma parameterizes by default; verify no raw concatenation is introduced).

- **F7 | MEDIUM | CWE-359 | IP/User-Agent are PII — retention stance required (GDPR).** ip_address (and UA) are personal data. The feature ships with NO purge job (orchestrator scope = OUT), but indefinite retention of PII without a documented basis is a compliance gap. Fix (document + ship): record a **retention STANCE in an ADR** (ADR-047) — audit logs are a legitimate-interest security/forensic record with a defined retention horizon (recommend a future scheduled purge of rows older than e.g. 365–730 days); user-deletion sets actor_id NULL (F4) but does NOT delete the row (forensic necessity overrides erasure for the security log — document this lawful-basis tension). Stance/ADR now, implementation later — not a blocker.

- **F8 | LOW | CWE-209 | Read-API error/response shape must not over-disclose.** Admin-only so disclosure risk is low, but: filter-API errors must use the existing sanitized global error handler (no stack traces), and the response DTO should expose only the intended audit fields (don't serialize internal row internals). Reuse the existing `ok()` envelope + sanitized error handler. LOW — note only.

### SEVERITY → ACTION mapping (per CLAUDE.md)
F1,F2,F3,F4 = HIGH → **FIX IT** (backend-dev/db-engineer MUST satisfy before quality-lead gate; security re-review at Gate-1). F5,F6,F7 = MEDIUM → document + ship with stated mitigation. F8 = LOW → log/note. **0 Critical → not BLOCKED.** All HIGH findings have clear, in-codebase-proven fix paths → STATUS DONE (forward requirements), not BLOCKED.

